I work in the Directorate General for  economic and financial affairs as an  economist I working on fiscal policy so  specifically on budgetary surveillance  and mainly on the implementation of the  stability and growth pact I did a  commercial engineering and then  afterwards a PhD in economics after the  PhD I started teaching first at the  University of Amsterdam and then I  switched to research at the National  Bank of Belgium in my job we need many  skills actually academic expertise work  on public finances we need a lot of  legal thinking because we are applying  law and sometimes we also had to make  law and we need of course a lot of  political awareness the European aspect  in particular interested me a lot first  of all because as an economist you can't  really get around the International  aspect and for European economies who  mainly trade and produce and integrate  with each other that means that there is  a naturally very strong European  dimension what fascinates me most on a  day-to-day basis is that I'm not only  learning through my work and through the  challenges that that are brought up but  I'm learning through the interaction  with people with interesting and really  knowledgeable people we work primarily  in English but we can also happen to  work in French and in the corridors for  sure you will hear tons of other  languages Spanish in Dalian and living  in Brussels is is really fun so I think  it's a really good sized town to be  living in it's big enough that there's a  lot to do both culturally but also  restaurants bars I really love working  in Brussels and living in Brussels I  think it's fantastic place I think it's  a very open and free minded and a modern  city the fact that there are so many  people working for the institutions but  also in jobs related to them means that  it's a very international City I think  working here is is a really fascinating  stimulating thing to do on a personal  level it's it's very rewarding because  you get to meet fantastic people from  all over the world and I think you can  contribute to something that that  ultimately touches upon all our lives  here in Europe
 

